    "body": "<img width=\"1675\" height=\"865\" alt=\"Image\" src=\"https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/64898441-61d5-481d-b1a9-abf63c4d2202\" alt=\"A screen capture showing the rendered Hampton by Hilton page and the browser’s DOM inspector side-by-side. Annotations highlight that the video element has had its role overridden to `image` and alt text has been provided via `aria-label`. A nearby pause button has been given a label of `Pause animation`. A VoiceOver speech viewer displays the full alt text for the video.\" />\n\n<hr>\n\n> I also tried `<video role=img aria-label=\"[description]\">`\n\nThis is the exact technique we’re using on the Hilton.com website for “[cinemagraphs](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cinemagraph)”, since they’re closer to an `image` than a `video` in terms of the content they express. In the cross-platform browser + assistive tech combos we’ve tested, this approach has performed well.\n\nThe`<video>` element is useful to us since we can use our full-featured custom video player to get other functions for little effort, such as a play/pause button, loop control, etc. We do customize the label of the play/pause button for cinemagraphs to “Play/pause _animation_” — it’s normally “Play/pause _video_”.\n\nHere are two examples where we use the same video player component to serve two different kinds of content:\n\n- [Hampton by Hilton](https://www.hilton.com/en/brands/hampton-by-hilton/) — serves an animated `image`-like “cinemagraph” with a simple text alternative.\n- [Graduate by Hilton](https://www.hilton.com/en/brands/graduate-hotels/) — serves a long-form synchronized video with captions, audio description, and descriptive transcript.",
