    "body": "The lack of `<video alt>` has come up as a blocker for https://github.com/whatwg/html/issues/11554 too.\n\n> Another common suggestion is to use [captions and subtitles](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/Media/Guides/Audio_and_video_delivery/Adding_captions_and_subtitles_to_HTML5_video), but those are comparatively time-consuming to generate, and are intended to accompany the media rather than provide a complete alternative.\n\nI think porting `<title>` into HTML as a way to have `title=\"\"` with markup is a great idea, but we shouldn't do it for one element only.\nAdditionally, `alt` and `title` have distinct purposes. Not all alt text is suitable as a tooltip. That said, an element is always more suitable for user-facing content, as it can be internationalized better, contain formatting markup, etc. If we were able to go back to the drawing board, we'd probably design `alt` as a child element of `<img>`.\n\nHowever, attributes _can_ be useful for making simple things easy, even if they're poor at making complex things possible. So, here's a radical idea: what about …_both_?\n- `<video alt=\"\">` for cases where that suffices\n- `<video><alt></alt></video>` for cases where markup/attributes are needed\n- We can even introduce `<alt>` for `<picture>` or all other content (since any content can have `role=img` and there is no way to provide alt text for it).\n\nIn some ways it's analogous to the pattern of having `src` for simple things and child `<source>` elements for more complex things or having both `aria-label` and `<label>`.\nThese don't have to be shipped all at once, e.g. `<video alt>` can be shipped first, which gives more time to figure out what `<alt>` should look like.\n",
